st D–E pair → only valid arrangement puts E at extreme right.\", \"questionText\": \"Five people A, B, C, D, E sit in a row facing north. B sits second to the right of A. C is not at any end. D is to the immediate left of E. A is not at an end. Who is at the extreme right?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-313\", \"title\": \"Question 2\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2397\", \"text\": \"26\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2398\", \"text\": \"25\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2399\", \"text\": \"24\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2400\", \"text\": \"23\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Bilal\'s top rank = 40 − 15 + 1 = 26 → after swap Ali takes that position.\", \"questionText\": \"In a class of 40 students, Ali is ranked 8th from top and Bilal is ranked 15th from bottom. If both swap positions, what will be Ali\'s new rank from top?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-314\", \"title\": \"Question 3\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2405\", \"text\": \"Son\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2406\", \"text\": \"Son-in-law\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2407\", \"text\": \"Brother\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2408\", \"text\": \"Father\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Z is X\'s daughter → W (husband of Z) becomes X\'s son-in-law.\", \"questionText\": \"Z is the sister of Y. Y is the son of X. W is the husband of Z. How is W related to X?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-315\", \"title\": \"Question 4\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2413\", \"text\": \"Only I follows\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2414\", \"text\": \"Only II follows\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2415\", \"text\": \"Both follow\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2416\", \"text\": \"None follow\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"No direct link between roses and red → both conclusions are assumptions, not guaranteed.\", \"questionText\": \"All roses are flowers. Some flowers are red. No red thing is cheap. Conclusion:\\nI. Some roses are not cheap\\nII. All roses are red\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-316\", \"title\": \"Question 5\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2421\", \"text\": \"A\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2422\", \"text\": \"B\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2423\", \"text\": \"C\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2424\", \"text\": \"D\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"From C &gt; E (and B not lowest), and A is between B and D → partial order forms: D &gt; A &gt; B &gt; C &gt; E. Since D is not highest (constraint), D shifts below a hidden top constraint-free position, but ordering stabilizes with A consistently occupying the second-highest valid position across all constraints.\", \"questionText\": \"Five students A, B, C, D, and E scored different marks in an exam.\\nA scored higher than B but lower than D\\nC scored higher than only E\\nB did not score the lowest\\nD is not the highest scorer\\nWho scored the second highest?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-317\", \"title\": \"Question 6\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2429\", \"text\": \"D\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2430\", \"text\": \"E\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2431\", \"text\": \"F\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2432\", \"text\": \"Cannot be determined\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Multiple valid circular arrangements satisfy conditions → opposite of C is not fixed.\", \"questionText\": \"Six persons sit in a circle facing center. A is opposite B. C is between A and D. E is not adjacent to B. Who is opposite C?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-318\", \"title\": \"Question 7\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2437\", \"text\": \"A\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2438\", \"text\": \"B\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2439\", \"text\": \"C\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2440\", \"text\": \"D\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Chain gives D &gt; A &gt; B ≥ C → D is strictly greatest.\", \"questionText\": \"A &gt; B, C &lt; D, B ≥ C, D &gt; A. Who is the greatest?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-319\", \"title\": \"Question 8\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2445\", \"text\": \"P true\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2446\", \"text\": \"Q true\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2447\", \"text\": \"P false\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2448\", \"text\": \"Cannot determine\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"R false contradicts \\\"Q false → R true\\\" → so Q must be true → hence P must be false.\", \"questionText\": \"When P is true, Q is false. When Q is false, R is true. R is false. Which is correct?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-320\", \"title\": \"Question 9\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2453\", \"text\": \"Only I\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2454\", \"text\": \"Only II\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2455\", \"text\": \"Both\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2456\", \"text\": \"None\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Some cats are dogs → all dogs are animals → some cats are animals. No animal is blue → some cats are not blue follows. But \\\"All cats are animals\\\" does not follow from \\\"some\\\".\", \"questionText\": \"Some cats are dogs. All dogs are animals. No animal is blue. Conclusion: I. Some cats are not blue II. All cats are animals\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-321\", \"title\": \"Question 10\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2461\", \"text\": \"starts\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2462\", \"text\": \"has started\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2463\", \"text\": \"had started\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2464\", \"text\": \"is starting\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Two past actions → the earlier one uses past perfect. Movie started before arrival → \\\"had started\\\" is logically correct.\", \"questionText\": \"Identify the correct form of the verb: By the time we arrived, the movie ______.\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-322\", \"title\": \"Question 11\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2469\", \"text\": \"do\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2470\", \"text\": \"did\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2471\", \"text\": \"am doing\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2472\", \"text\": \"have done\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"\\\"Right now\\\" signals present continuous tense. So the correct structure is \\\"am/is/are + verb + ing\\\".\", \"questionText\": \"Choose the correct option: I ______ my homework right now.\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-323\", \"title\": \"Question 12\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2477\", \"text\": \"works\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2478\", \"text\": \"worked\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2479\", \"text\": \"has worked\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2480\", \"text\": \"is working\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"\\\"Since 2020\\\" indicates action started in past and continues → present perfect. Hence \\\"has worked\\\" is correct.\", \"questionText\": \"Fill in the blank with correct tense: She ______ in this company since 2020.\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-324\", \"title\": \"Question 13\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2485\", \"text\": \"go\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2486\", \"text\": \"went\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2487\", \"text\": \"are going\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2488\", \"text\": \"have gone\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Planned future actions often use present continuous. \\\"Are going\\\" correctly expresses a scheduled future plan.\", \"questionText\": \"Choose the correct future tense: We ______ to Lahore next week.\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-325\", \"title\": \"Question 14\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2493\", \"text\": \"we had started\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2494\", \"text\": \"had we started\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2495\", \"text\": \"we started\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2496\", \"text\": \"did we start\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"\\\"Hardly\\\" at the beginning → inversion required. Use past perfect + inversion → \\\"had we started\\\".\", \"questionText\": \"Identify the correct verb form: Hardly ______ the meeting when the manager interrupted.\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-326\", \"title\": \"Question 15\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2501\", \"text\": \"leaves\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2502\", \"text\": \"left\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2503\", \"text\": \"had left\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2504\", \"text\": \"has left\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Third conditional → past unreal situation. Structure: If + past perfect → would have + past participle.\", \"questionText\": \"Choose the correct option: If she ______ earlier, she would have caught the train.\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-327\", \"title\": \"Question 16\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2509\", \"text\": \"will complete\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2510\", \"text\": \"will have completed\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2511\", \"text\": \"will have been completing\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2512\", \"text\": \"have completed\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"\\\"By next year\\\" + duration → future perfect is preferred for completion before a future point.\", \"questionText\": \"Fill in the blank correctly: By next year, I ______ this course for five years.\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-328\", \"title\": \"Question 17\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2517\", \"text\": \"studies\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2518\", \"text\": \"has been studying\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2519\", \"text\": \"had been studying\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2520\", \"text\": \"was studying\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Action continued before another past event → past perfect continuous fits best.\", \"questionText\": \"Choose the most accurate tense: He ______ for two hours when the lights suddenly went out.\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784891579277-329\", \"title\": \"Question 18\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2525\", \"text\": \"was analyzed, had, been submitted\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2526\", \"text\": \"had been analyzed, was, submitted\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2527\", \"text\": \"was being analyzed, had, been submitted\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784891579277-2528\", \"text\": \"had been analyzed, had, been submitted\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The discovery (\\\"realized\\\") is past, but the incorrect action was ongoing before that → past continuous passive (\\\"was being analyzed\\\"). The submission was completed earlier than the realization → past perfect passive (\\\"had been submitted\\\").\", \"questionText\": \"Choose the correct option: \\\"By the time the researcher realized that the data ______ incorrectly, the paper ______ already ______ for publication.\\\"\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}], \"storageKey\": \"15\", \"totalPoints\": 18}',1,5,'2026-07-24 11:13:04','2026-07-24 11:14:29',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-24 11:14:29',1),(60,65,'{\"testId\": 65, \"savedAt\": \"2026-07-25T15:22:28.636Z\", \"version\": 1, \"questions\": [{\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1905\", \"title\": \"Question 1\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15009\", \"text\": \"is\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15010\", \"text\": \"are\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15011\", \"text\": \"was\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15012\", \"text\": \"have been\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"When a compound subject uses \'neither...nor,\' the verb agrees with the subject closest to it—\'members\' is plural, requiring the plural verb \'are.\' Options A and C are singular and incorrectly match the distant subject; D incorrectly suggests present perfect where simple present is required.\", \"questionText\": \"Neither the Dean nor the faculty members ______ satisfied with the new curriculum\'s implementation timeline.\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1906\", \"title\": \"Question 2\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15017\", \"text\": \"The patient\'s prognosis was significantly better than anticipated.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15018\", \"text\": \"The medication\'s side effects were less severe then expected.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15019\", \"text\": \"The research findings were disseminated through multiple peer-reviewed journals.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15020\", \"text\": \"The surgeon\'s meticulous technique ensured minimal blood loss.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"\'Then\' is incorrectly used for comparison; the correct word is \'than.\' Option A correctly uses the possessive form, C uses the correct spelling of \'disseminated,\' and D is grammatically flawless.\", \"questionText\": \"Which of the following sentences contains an error in word function or spelling?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1907\", \"title\": \"Question 3\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15025\", \"text\": \"will have been completing\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15026\", \"text\": \"will have completed\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15027\", \"text\": \"has completed\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15028\", \"text\": \"will complete\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The future perfect tense (\'will have completed\') correctly indicates an action that will be finished before a specified future time. Option A overemphasizes the duration unnecessarily; C uses present perfect incorrectly for future reference; D lacks the completion aspect required by \'by the time.\'\", \"questionText\": \"By the time the consultant arrives at the emergency department, the trauma team ______ the initial assessment for over thirty minutes.\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1908\", \"title\": \"Question 4\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15033\", \"text\": \"Criticize pharmaceutical companies for inadequate research funding\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15034\", \"text\": \"Advocate for a shift in medical strategy toward prevention and stewardship\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15035\", \"text\": \"Present statistical evidence regarding antibiotic resistance rates\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15036\", \"text\": \"Explain the biological mechanisms behind bacterial evolution\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The passage builds an argument culminating in the recommendation to prioritize stewardship and prevention, making B the correct purpose. Option A misrepresents the target of criticism; C incorrectly claims statistical evidence is presented; D focuses on a detail not addressed in the passage.\", \"questionText\": \"The increasing prevalence of antibiotic-resistant pathogens represents one of the most significant threats to modern medical practice. Despite decades of research and substantial financial investment, the development of novel antimicrobial agents has failed to keep pace with bacterial evolution. The medical community must therefore reconsider its approach, prioritizing antimicrobial stewardship and infection prevention rather than relying solely on pharmaceutical interventions. The author\'s primary purpose in this passage is to:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1909\", \"title\": \"Question 5\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15041\", \"text\": \"Encouraging\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15042\", \"text\": \"Predicted\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15043\", \"text\": \"Deviant\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15044\", \"text\": \"Insignificant\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"\'Anomalous\' means deviating from the normal or expected pattern, making \'deviant\' the closest contextual match. Option A suggests positivity not implied; B contradicts \'necessitating reassessment\'; D is unrelated to the meaning of the term.\", \"questionText\": \"In the sentence, \\\"The preliminary results were anomalous, necessitating a complete reassessment of the experimental protocol,\\\" the underlined word most nearly means:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1910\", \"title\": \"Question 6\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15049\", \"text\": \"The attending physician, after reviewing the patient\'s history, decided to proceed with the diagnostic procedure.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15050\", \"text\": \"To administer the medication correctly, the patient\'s weight must be accurately measured.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15051\", \"text\": \"Running through the hospital corridor, the resident\'s stethoscope fell to the floor.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15052\", \"text\": \"Having completed the rounds, the medical team convened for the morning briefing.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"This is a dangling modifier—the introductory phrase \'Running through the hospital corridor\' logically modifies the resident, but the subject is \'stethoscope,\' creating illogical modification. Options A, B, and D all have correctly attached modifiers.\", \"questionText\": \"Identify the sentence that contains a faulty structure:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1911\", \"title\": \"Question 7\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15057\", \"text\": \"No error\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15058\", \"text\": \"Change \'their\' to \'his or her\'\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15059\", \"text\": \"Change \'they\' to \'he or she\'\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15060\", \"text\": \"Change \'their\' to \'our\'\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"\'Every medical student\' is grammatically singular, requiring the singular pronoun \'his or her\'; the use of \'their\' and \'they\' creates a pronoun-antecedent agreement error. Option C only partially fixes the issue; D incorrectly changes to first person without addressing the agreement problem.\", \"questionText\": \"Every medical student must complete their clinical rotations before they can apply for residency positions.\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1912\", \"title\": \"Question 8\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15065\", \"text\": \"to use, to reduce\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15066\", \"text\": \"to use, reducing\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15067\", \"text\": \"using, to reduce\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15068\", \"text\": \"using, reducing\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"\'Decision\' is correctly followed by the infinitive \'to use,\' and \'proved\' requires the infinitive \'to reduce\' to express the result. Option B incorrectly pairs the second verb with a gerund; C and D misuse \'using\' after \'decision,\' which requires the infinitive form.\", \"questionText\": \"The surgeon\'s decision ______ the minimally invasive approach proved ______ the recovery time significantly.\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1913\", \"title\": \"Question 9\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15073\", \"text\": \"The patient\'s symptoms included fever, chills, and, persistent fatigue.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15074\", \"text\": \"The consultant asked, \\\"Have you reviewed the latest lab results\\\"?\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15075\", \"text\": \"The study—which involved 500 participants—demonstrated a significant correlation.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15076\", \"text\": \"The attending physician ordered: CBC, electrolytes, and cardiac enzymes.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The em dashes correctly set off the nonrestrictive clause. Option A contains an unnecessary comma after \'and\'; B incorrectly places the question mark outside the quotation; D misuses a colon after a verb, which is grammatically incorrect.\", \"questionText\": \"Choose the correctly punctuated sentence:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1914\", \"title\": \"Question 10\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15081\", \"text\": \"U\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15082\", \"text\": \"V\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15083\", \"text\": \"W\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15084\", \"text\": \"T\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The pattern adds consecutive increments of +2, +3, +4, +5, and then +6 to the letter positions (B=2 → D=4 [+2], D=4 → G=7 [+3], G=7 → K=11 [+4], K=11 → P=16 [+5], P=16 → V=22 [+6]). Options A, C, and D do not follow this sequential increment pattern.\", \"questionText\": \"Consider the following series: B, D, G, K, P, ? What comes next in the sequence?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1915\", \"title\": \"Question 11\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15089\", \"text\": \"Only I follows\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15090\", \"text\": \"Only II follows\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15091\", \"text\": \"Both I and II follow\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15092\", \"text\": \"Neither I nor II follows\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Conclusion II follows because some health professionals are researchers, and no researchers are surgeons, so at least those health professionals are not surgeons. Conclusion I does not follow—\'All doctors are health professionals\' and \'Some health professionals are researchers\' does not guarantee any doctor is a researcher. This is the classic fallacy of the undistributed middle term.\", \"questionText\": \"Statements: - All doctors are health professionals. - Some health professionals are researchers. - No researcher is a surgeon. Conclusions: I. Some doctors are researchers. II. Some health professionals are not surgeons. Which conclusion(s) logically follow(s)?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1916\", \"title\": \"Question 12\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15097\", \"text\": \"Emergency department overcrowding causes heat-related illnesses.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15098\", \"text\": \"High temperatures cause increased emergency department visits.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15099\", \"text\": \"Increased admissions cause higher average temperatures.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15100\", \"text\": \"The five-year study period is the cause of the overcrowding.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The data clearly establishes a temporal and correlational link where high temperatures precede and logically lead to more heat-related illnesses and subsequent ED visits. Option A reverses causation; C is entirely illogical; D confuses the period of observation with a causal factor.\", \"questionText\": \"A study conducted over five years in a major metropolitan hospital found that emergency department overcrowding significantly increased during months when the average temperature exceeded 38°C. Additionally, patient admission rates for heat-related illnesses rose by 67% during these periods. Which of the following is the most logical cause-and-effect relationship?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1917\", \"title\": \"Question 13\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15105\", \"text\": \"Immediately suspend all surgical procedures until further investigation.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15106\", \"text\": \"Conduct a comprehensive review of sterilization procedures and staff compliance.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15107\", \"text\": \"Blame the nursing staff for negligence in protocol implementation.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15108\", \"text\": \"Increase the number of surgical beds to accommodate more patients.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"A systematic review to identify the underlying cause is the logical first step—it addresses the problem without premature action. Option A is extreme and impractical; C assigns blame without evidence; D is unrelated to addressing the infection rate problem.\", \"questionText\": \"Statement: A recent audit of a tertiary care hospital revealed that post-operative infection rates in the surgical ward have increased by 40% over the past six months, despite standard sterilization protocols being followed. Which course of action is most logically warranted?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1918\", \"title\": \"Question 14\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15113\", \"text\": \"Ali, Chaman, Danish, Bilal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15114\", \"text\": \"Ali, Danish, Chaman, Bilal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15115\", \"text\": \"Danish, Ali, Chaman, Bilal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15116\", \"text\": \"Danish, Chaman, Ali, Bilal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"From the clues: Ali &lt; Bilal, Danish &lt; Chaman, and Chaman &lt; Bilal. Combining Danish &lt; Chaman &lt; Bilal and Ali &lt; Bilal, we get Ali and Danish before Chaman and Bilal, with Bilal last. The only order satisfying all conditions is Ali, Danish, Chaman, Bilal. Options A, C, and D violate at least one relationship.\", \"questionText\": \"In a hospital ward, four patients—Ali, Bilal, Chaman, and Danish—are scheduled for surgeries. Ali\'s surgery is before Bilal\'s. Chaman\'s surgery is after Danish\'s. Bilal\'s surgery is after Chaman\'s. What is the correct order of surgeries from first to last?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1919\", \"title\": \"Question 15\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15121\", \"text\": \"The claim is true because a clinical trial is a scientific study.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15122\", \"text\": \"The sample size is too small and the duration too short to establish safety.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15123\", \"text\": \"The painkiller must be effective because no side effects were reported.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15124\", \"text\": \"The company is hiding information about side effects.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The criticism correctly identifies methodological limitations—50 participants for six months is insufficient to establish safety; rare side effects require larger populations and longer durations. Option A incorrectly validates a flawed claim; C commits the fallacy of equating absence of side effects with efficacy; D makes an unsupported accusation.\", \"questionText\": \"A pharmaceutical company claims, \\\"Our newly developed painkiller is 100% safe because no side effects were reported during the six-month clinical trial involving 50 participants.\\\" Which of the following represents the strongest logical criticism of this claim?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1920\", \"title\": \"Question 16\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15129\", \"text\": \"28\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15130\", \"text\": \"30\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15131\", \"text\": \"32\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15132\", \"text\": \"36\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The pattern follows n(n+1) or the sequence of adding consecutive even numbers: +4, +6, +8, +10, +12. Following this, 20 + 10 = 30. Option A (28) would require adding 8 instead of 10; C and D similarly deviate from the established pattern of increasing additions.\", \"questionText\": \"Find the missing number in the series: 2, 6, 12, 20, ?, 42\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1921\", \"title\": \"Question 17\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15137\", \"text\": \"Some substances are not medications.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15138\", \"text\": \"All natural things are substances.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15139\", \"text\": \"Some medications are poisonous.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15140\", \"text\": \"All substances are medications.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Since all medications are substances, but not all substances are medications (as some substances are poisonous and some substances are natural), it logically follows that some substances are not medications. Option B cannot be concluded from the premises; C directly contradicts \'No medication is natural\' (since natural is not addressed in the relationship); D reverses the conditional relationship.\", \"questionText\": \"Statements: All medications are substances. Some substances are poisonous. No medication is natural. Which of the following can be logically concluded?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1784992941789-1922\", \"title\": \"Question 18\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15145\", \"text\": \"Proposal X, because it generates more revenue over 10 years than its cost.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15146\", \"text\": \"Proposal Y, because it costs less than Proposal X.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15147\", \"text\": \"Proposal X, because it provides equipment that lasts longer.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1784992941789-15148\", \"text\": \"Proposal Y, because it improves patient satisfaction.\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Proposal X generates 100 million rupees over 10 years (10M × 10) against a 50M investment, yielding a 50M net return; Proposal Y costs 40M over 5 years with no specified financial return, making X more financially viable. Option B ignores the revenue generation aspect; C makes an unsupported claim about equipment longevity; D discusses patient satisfaction, not financial viability.\", \"questionText\": \"A hospital administrator must decide between two budget proposals: - Proposal X: Invest in new diagnostic equipment costing 50 million rupees, which will increase patient throughput by 15% and generate additional annual revenue of 10 million rupees for the next 10 years. - Proposal Y: Hire 15 additional nurses costing 40 million rupees in salaries over 5 years, which will reduce average patient waiting time from 45 to 20 minutes. Based purely on long-term financial returns, which proposal is more viable?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}], \"storageKey\": \"65\", \"totalPoints\": 18}',1,5,'2026-07-25 15:22:28','2026-07-25 15:23:06',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 15:23:06',1),(61,67,'{\"testId\": 67, \"savedAt\": \"2026-07-25T18:38:10.202Z\", \"version\": 1, \"questions\": [{\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-3\", \"title\": \"Question 1\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-9\", \"text\": \"Joule\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-10\", \"text\": \"Watt\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-11\", \"text\": \"Newton\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-12\", \"text\": \"Pascal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The SI unit of force is the Newton (N), named after Isaac Newton.\", \"questionText\": \"<p>What is the SI unit of force? </p><img src=\\\"/api/uploads/question-bank/614347502c167f62787432486a70f4ec10ff72bc22ab419b.jpg\\\" />\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-4\", \"title\": \"Question 2\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-17\", \"text\": \"Barometer\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-18\", \"text\": \"Thermometer\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-19\", \"text\": \"Speedometer\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-20\", \"text\": \"Ammeter\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"A thermometer is used to measure the temperature of an object or substance.\", \"questionText\": \"Which instrument is used to measure temperature?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-5\", \"title\": \"Question 3\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-25\", \"text\": \"Gram\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-26\", \"text\": \"Kilogram\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-27\", \"text\": \"Pound\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-28\", \"text\": \"Ton\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The kilogram (kg) is the standard SI unit used to measure mass.\", \"questionText\": \"What is the SI unit of mass?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-6\", \"title\": \"Question 4\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-33\", \"text\": \"Magnetic force\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-34\", \"text\": \"Electric force\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-35\", \"text\": \"Gravity\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-36\", \"text\": \"Friction\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Gravity is the force that attracts objects toward the center of the Earth.\", \"questionText\": \"Which force pulls objects toward the Earth?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-7\", \"title\": \"Question 5\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-41\", \"text\": \"Minute\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-42\", \"text\": \"Hour\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-43\", \"text\": \"Second\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-44\", \"text\": \"Day\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The second (s) is the SI base unit of time.\", \"questionText\": \"What is the SI unit of time?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-8\", \"title\": \"Question 6\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-49\", \"text\": \"Heat\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-50\", \"text\": \"Length\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-51\", \"text\": \"Mass\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-52\", \"text\": \"Density\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Heat is a form of energy that can be transferred between objects.\", \"questionText\": \"Which of the following is a form of energy?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-9\", \"title\": \"Question 7\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-57\", \"text\": \"100 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-58\", \"text\": \"50 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-59\", \"text\": \"10 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-60\", \"text\": \"0 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"An object at rest has a speed of zero meters per second.\", \"questionText\": \"What is the speed of an object if it is not moving?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-10\", \"title\": \"Question 8\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-65\", \"text\": \"Liquid\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-66\", \"text\": \"Gas\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-67\", \"text\": \"Solid\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-68\", \"text\": \"Plasma\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Solids maintain both their shape and volume under normal conditions.\", \"questionText\": \"Which state of matter has a fixed shape and fixed volume?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-11\", \"title\": \"Question 9\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-73\", \"text\": \"Meter\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-74\", \"text\": \"Kilometer\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-75\", \"text\": \"Centimeter\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-76\", \"text\": \"Inch\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The meter (m) is the SI unit used to measure length.\", \"questionText\": \"What is the SI unit of length?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-12\", \"title\": \"Question 10\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-81\", \"text\": \"Albert Einstein\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-82\", \"text\": \"Galileo Galilei\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-83\", \"text\": \"Isaac Newton\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-84\", \"text\": \"Nikola Tesla\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Isaac Newton developed the law of universal gravitation.\", \"questionText\": \"Which scientist is famous for the law of gravity?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-13\", \"title\": \"Question 11\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-89\", \"text\": \"Newton\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-90\", \"text\": \"Joule\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-91\", \"text\": \"Watt\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-92\", \"text\": \"Volt\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The joule (J) is the SI unit used to measure energy.\", \"questionText\": \"What is the SI unit of energy?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-14\", \"title\": \"Question 12\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-97\", \"text\": \"Sound\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-98\", \"text\": \"Water waves\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-99\", \"text\": \"Light\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-100\", \"text\": \"Seismic waves\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Light can travel through empty space, while sound requires a medium.\", \"questionText\": \"Which of the following can travel through a vacuum?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-15\", \"title\": \"Question 13\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-105\", \"text\": \"Volt\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-106\", \"text\": \"Ohm\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-107\", \"text\": \"Ampere\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-108\", \"text\": \"Watt\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The ampere (A) is the SI unit used to measure electric current.\", \"questionText\": \"What is the SI unit of electric current?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-16\", \"title\": \"Question 14\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-113\", \"text\": \"Solid\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-114\", \"text\": \"Liquid\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-115\", \"text\": \"Gas\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-116\", \"text\": \"Plasma\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Liquids have a fixed volume but flow to take the shape of their container.\", \"questionText\": \"Which state of matter takes the shape of its container but has a fixed volume?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-17\", \"title\": \"Question 15\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-121\", \"text\": \"A force that opposes motion\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-122\", \"text\": \"A type of energy\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-123\", \"text\": \"A unit of power\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-124\", \"text\": \"A measuring instrument\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Friction is a force that resists the movement of objects across surfaces.\", \"questionText\": \"QUESTION: What is friction?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-18\", \"title\": \"Question 16\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-129\", \"text\": \"Meter per second\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-130\", \"text\": \"Kilometer per hour\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-131\", \"text\": \"Mile per hour\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-132\", \"text\": \"Centimeter per second\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Speed is measured in meters per second (m/s) in the SI system.\", \"questionText\": \"What is the SI unit of speed?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-19\", \"title\": \"Question 17\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-137\", \"text\": \"Earth\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-138\", \"text\": \"Mars\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-139\", \"text\": \"Jupiter\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-140\", \"text\": \"Venus\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Jupiter is the largest planet in the solar system and has the strongest gravity among the planets.\", \"questionText\": \"Which planet has the strongest gravitational pull among the planets in our solar system?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-20\", \"title\": \"Question 18\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-145\", \"text\": \"50°C\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-146\", \"text\": \"75°C\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-147\", \"text\": \"100°C\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-148\", \"text\": \"150°C\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Pure water boils at 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure.\", \"questionText\": \"What is the boiling point of pure water at standard atmospheric pressure?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-21\", \"title\": \"Question 19\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-153\", \"text\": \"Kinetic Energy\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-154\", \"text\": \"Elastic Potential Energy\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-155\", \"text\": \"Thermal Energy\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-156\", \"text\": \"Sound Energy\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"A stretched rubber band stores elastic potential energy due to its deformation.\", \"questionText\": \"Which type of energy is stored in a stretched rubber band?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785004621100-22\", \"title\": \"Question 20\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-161\", \"text\": \"Joule\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-162\", \"text\": \"Newton\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-163\", \"text\": \"Watt\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785004621100-164\", \"text\": \"Pascal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The watt (W) is the SI unit of power and measures the rate of energy transfer.\", \"questionText\": \"What is the SI unit of power?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}], \"storageKey\": \"67\", \"totalPoints\": 20}',4,5,'2026-07-25 18:37:00','2026-07-25 18:38:18',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:38:18',4),(62,68,'{\"testId\": 68, \"savedAt\": \"2026-07-26T15:19:11.822Z\", \"version\": 1, \"questions\": [{\"id\": \"question-1785079144448-3\", \"title\": \"Question 1\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-9\", \"text\": \"Dilute H₂SO₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-10\", \"text\": \"Hg(OAc)₂/H₂O followed by NaBH₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-11\", \"text\": \"BH₃·THF followed by H₂O₂/OH⁻\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-12\", \"text\": \"Conc. H₂SO₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Hydroboration-oxidation (BH₃·THF followed by H₂O₂/OH⁻) gives anti-Markovnikov alcohol without carbocation rearrangement.\", \"questionText\": \"Which reagent converts an alkene into an alcohol through anti-Markovnikov hydration?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144448-4\", \"title\": \"Question 2\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-17\", \"text\": \"Methanol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-18\", \"text\": \"Ethanol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-19\", \"text\": \"Propan-1-ol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-20\", \"text\": \"Propan-2-ol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Reduction of an aldehyde produces a primary alcohol. Acetaldehyde (CH₃CHO) gives ethanol (CH₃CH₂OH).\", \"questionText\": \"Which alcohol is obtained when acetaldehyde is reduced with NaBH₄?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144448-5\", \"title\": \"Question 3\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-25\", \"text\": \"Hydration of 2-methylpropene with dilute H₂SO₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-26\", \"text\": \"Reaction of methylmagnesium bromide with acetone followed by hydrolysis\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-27\", \"text\": \"Hydrolysis of tert-butyl chloride with aqueous KOH\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-28\", \"text\": \"Reduction of tert-butanal with LiAlH₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Reduction of tert-butanal forms a primary alcohol, not tert-butyl alcohol.\", \"questionText\": \"Which reaction is least suitable for preparing tert-butyl alcohol?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144448-6\", \"title\": \"Question 4\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-33\", \"text\": \"HCl/ZnCl₂\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-34\", \"text\": \"SOCl₂\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-35\", \"text\": \"Dilute H₂SO₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-36\", \"text\": \"NaOH\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Thionyl chloride (SOCl₂) converts alcohols to alkyl chlorides efficiently with minimal rearrangement.\", \"questionText\": \"Which reagent converts an alcohol directly into the corresponding alkyl chloride without forming carbocation intermediates?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144448-7\", \"title\": \"Question 5\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-41\", \"text\": \"Diethyl ether\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-42\", \"text\": \"Ethanal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-43\", \"text\": \"Ethene\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-44\", \"text\": \"Ethyl hydrogen sulfate\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"At about 170°C, concentrated H₂SO₄ causes intramolecular dehydration of ethanol to ethene.\", \"questionText\": \"Ethanol on heating with concentrated H₂SO₄ at about 170°C mainly forms:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144448-8\", \"title\": \"Question 6\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-49\", \"text\": \"Methanol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-50\", \"text\": \"Ethanol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-51\", \"text\": \"Propan-2-ol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-52\", \"text\": \"2-Methylpropan-2-ol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Secondary alcohols are oxidized to ketones. Propan-2-ol gives propanone.\", \"questionText\": \"Which alcohol gives a ketone on oxidation with acidified K₂Cr₂O₇?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144448-9\", \"title\": \"Question 7\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-57\", \"text\": \"HBr → KOH (aq)\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-58\", \"text\": \"BH₃·THF → H₂O₂/OH⁻\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-59\", \"text\": \"O₃/Zn\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-60\", \"text\": \"KMnO₄/H⁺\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Hydroboration-oxidation adds water across the double bond to produce ethanol from ethene.\", \"questionText\": \"Which sequence correctly prepares ethanol from ethene?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144448-10\", \"title\": \"Question 8\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-65\", \"text\": \"Propan-1-ol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-66\", \"text\": \"Butan-2-ol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-67\", \"text\": \"2-Methylpropan-2-ol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-68\", \"text\": \"Ethanol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Tertiary alcohols lack a hydrogen on the carbon bearing the –OH group and resist oxidation under ordinary conditions.\", \"questionText\": \"Which alcohol does not undergo oxidation with acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ under ordinary conditions?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144448-11\", \"title\": \"Question 9\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-73\", \"text\": \"Sodium acetate\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-74\", \"text\": \"Sodium ethoxide and hydrogen gas\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-75\", \"text\": \"Ethene and water\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-76\", \"text\": \"Ethyl chloride\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Alcohols react with sodium metal to form sodium alkoxide and hydrogen gas. Reaction: 2C₂H₅OH + 2Na → 2C₂H₅ONa + H₂\", \"questionText\": \"Which product is formed when ethanol reacts with sodium metal?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144448-12\", \"title\": \"Question 10\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-81\", \"text\": \"Formaldehyde followed by H₃O⁺\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-82\", \"text\": \"Acetaldehyde followed by H₃O⁺\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-83\", \"text\": \"Acetone followed by H₃O⁺\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-84\", \"text\": \"CO₂ followed by H₃O⁺\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Grignard reagent reacts with formaldehyde to give a primary alcohol containing one extra carbon. Phenylmagnesium bromide forms benzyl alcohol after acidic hydrolysis.\", \"questionText\": \"Which reagent is most suitable for converting phenylmagnesium bromide into benzyl alcohol?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144448-13\", \"title\": \"Question 11\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-89\", \"text\": \"Chlorobenzene + aqueous NaOH at 623 K and 300 atm followed by acidification\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-90\", \"text\": \"Chlorobenzene + alcoholic KOH\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-91\", \"text\": \"Chlorobenzene + NaNH₂ in liquid NH₃\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-92\", \"text\": \"Chlorobenzene + Zn dust\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Industrially, phenol is prepared by the Dow process, where chlorobenzene reacts with aqueous NaOH under high temperature and pressure to form sodium phenoxide, which on acidification gives phenol.\", \"questionText\": \"Which reaction is specifically used for the industrial preparation of phenol from chlorobenzene?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144448-14\", \"title\": \"Question 12\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-97\", \"text\": \"Chlorobenzene\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-98\", \"text\": \"Phenol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-99\", \"text\": \"Benzyl alcohol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-100\", \"text\": \"Aniline\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Benzene diazonium chloride undergoes hydrolysis with warm water to directly produce phenol with the evolution of N₂ gas.\", \"questionText\": \"During the preparation of phenol from benzene diazonium chloride, the immediate product of hydrolysis is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-15\", \"title\": \"Question 13\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144448-105\", \"text\": \"Dilute HNO₃\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-106\", \"text\": \"Bromine water\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-107\", \"text\": \"No common reagent can do so\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-108\", \"text\": \"NaOH(aq)\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Benzene cannot be converted directly into phenol in one step because the aromatic ring is highly stable. Industrial or multistep methods are required.\", \"questionText\": \"Which reagent converts benzene directly into phenol in a single step?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-16\", \"title\": \"Question 14\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-113\", \"text\": \"Nitration with concentrated HNO₃ only\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-114\", \"text\": \"Bromination with bromine water\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-115\", \"text\": \"Reduction with Zn dust\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-116\", \"text\": \"Oxidation with KMnO₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The –OH group strongly activates the benzene ring, allowing bromination with bromine water without FeBr₃ catalyst to form 2,4,6-tribromophenol.\", \"questionText\": \"Which reaction of phenol proceeds through electrophilic aromatic substitution without requiring a catalyst?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-17\", \"title\": \"Question 15\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-121\", \"text\": \"Bromobenzene\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-122\", \"text\": \"2-Bromophenol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-123\", \"text\": \"2,4-Dibromophenol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-124\", \"text\": \"2,4,6-Tribromophenol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The activating effect of the –OH group leads to rapid substitution at both ortho positions and the para position, giving 2,4,6-tribromophenol.\", \"questionText\": \"When phenol is treated with excess bromine water, the major product is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-18\", \"title\": \"Question 16\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-129\", \"text\": \"Reimer–Tiemann reagent (CHCl₃/NaOH)\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-130\", \"text\": \"Kolbe reagent (CO₂/NaOH)\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-131\", \"text\": \"Dilute H₂SO₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-132\", \"text\": \"Zn/HCl\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"In the Reimer–Tiemann reaction, phenol reacts with chloroform and NaOH to produce salicylaldehyde after acidification.\", \"questionText\": \"Which reagent converts phenol into salicylaldehyde?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-19\", \"title\": \"Question 17\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-137\", \"text\": \"Benzoic acid\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-138\", \"text\": \"Salicylic acid\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-139\", \"text\": \"Picric acid\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-140\", \"text\": \"Phthalic acid\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"This is the Kolbe–Schmitt reaction. Sodium phenoxide reacts with CO₂ under pressure to form sodium salicylate, which yields salicylic acid on acidification.\", \"questionText\": \"Phenol reacts with carbon dioxide in the presence of NaOH under pressure to give, after acidification:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-20\", \"title\": \"Question 18\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-145\", \"text\": \"Zn dust\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-146\", \"text\": \"NaOH\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-147\", \"text\": \"Br₂/H₂O\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-148\", \"text\": \"CHCl₃/NaOH\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Heating phenol with zinc dust removes the oxygen atom as ZnO, converting phenol into benzene.\", \"questionText\": \"Which reagent converts phenol into benzene?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-21\", \"title\": \"Question 19\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-153\", \"text\": \"o-Nitrophenol only\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-154\", \"text\": \"p-Nitrophenol only\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-155\", \"text\": \"2,4,6-Trinitrophenol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-156\", \"text\": \"Nitrobenzene\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Strong nitrating conditions introduce three nitro groups to form 2,4,6-trinitrophenol (picric acid).\", \"questionText\": \"Which product is formed when phenol is nitrated with concentrated HNO₃ and concentrated H₂SO₄?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-22\", \"title\": \"Question 20\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-161\", \"text\": \"The –OH group withdraws electrons by induction only\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-162\", \"text\": \"The –OH group increases electron density on the ring through resonance\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-163\", \"text\": \"Bromine is a stronger electrophile than chlorine\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-164\", \"text\": \"Phenol first forms benzene before bromination\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The lone pair on oxygen is delocalized into the benzene ring by resonance, increasing electron density at the ortho and para positions and making electrophilic substitution much faster than in benzene.\", \"questionText\": \"Which statement correctly explains why phenol undergoes bromination much faster than benzene?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-23\", \"title\": \"Question 21\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-169\", \"text\": \"LiAlH₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-170\", \"text\": \"H₂/Pd\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-171\", \"text\": \"H₂, Pd/BaSO₄ poisoned with quinoline\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-172\", \"text\": \"NaBH₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Rosenmund reduction uses H₂ with Pd/BaSO₄ poisoned by quinoline to selectively reduce acid chlorides to aldehydes.\", \"questionText\": \"Which reagent converts an acid chloride into an aldehyde without further reduction?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-24\", \"title\": \"Question 22\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-177\", \"text\": \"LiAlH₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-178\", \"text\": \"DIBAL-H (1 equivalent, low temperature)\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-179\", \"text\": \"NaBH₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-180\", \"text\": \"KMnO₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"DIBAL-H partially reduces nitriles to imines, which on hydrolysis give aldehydes.\", \"questionText\": \"Which reagent converts a nitrile directly into an aldehyde after hydrolysis?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-25\", \"title\": \"Question 23\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-185\", \"text\": \"Cannizzaro reaction\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-186\", \"text\": \"Clemmensen reduction\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-187\", \"text\": \"Dry distillation of calcium carboxylates\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-188\", \"text\": \"Rosenmund reduction\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Heating calcium salts of carboxylic acids produces symmetrical ketones.\", \"questionText\": \"Which reaction converts calcium salts of carboxylic acids into ketones on dry distillation?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-26\", \"title\": \"Question 24\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-193\", \"text\": \"CH₃CHO\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-194\", \"text\": \"C₂H₅CHO\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-195\", \"text\": \"C₆H₅CHO\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-196\", \"text\": \"CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Benzaldehyde lacks an α-hydrogen, so it cannot undergo aldol condensation.\", \"questionText\": \"Which aldehyde does not undergo aldol condensation?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-27\", \"title\": \"Question 25\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-201\", \"text\": \"Gem-diol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-202\", \"text\": \"Acetal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-203\", \"text\": \"Hemiacetal only\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-204\", \"text\": \"Ester\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Aldehydes react with excess alcohol under acidic conditions to form acetals through a hemiacetal intermediate.\", \"questionText\": \"Which product is formed when acetaldehyde is treated with excess alcohol in the presence of dry HCl?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-28\", \"title\": \"Question 26\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-209\", \"text\": \"Wolff–Kishner reagent\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-210\", \"text\": \"Zn(Hg)/conc. HCl\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-211\", \"text\": \"NaBH₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-212\", \"text\": \"PCC\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Clemmensen reduction uses Zn(Hg)/conc. HCl to reduce the carbonyl group to –CH₂–.\", \"questionText\": \"Which reagent converts aldehydes and ketones into hydrocarbons under strongly acidic conditions?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-29\", \"title\": \"Question 27\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-217\", \"text\": \"Acetone\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-218\", \"text\": \"3-Pentanone\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-219\", \"text\": \"2-Butanone\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-220\", \"text\": \"Benzophenone\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Oxidative cleavage of 2-butanone produces acetic acid and formic acid (which is further oxidized to CO₂ under strong conditions).\", \"questionText\": \"Which ketone gives two different carboxylic acids on vigorous oxidation?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-30\", \"title\": \"Question 28\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-225\", \"text\": \"Clemmensen reduction\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-226\", \"text\": \"Aldol condensation\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-227\", \"text\": \"Grignard reaction followed by oxidation\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-228\", \"text\": \"Cyanohydrin formation followed by hydrolysis and reduction\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Cyanohydrin formation introduces one extra carbon. Subsequent hydrolysis and reduction convert it into a higher aldehyde.\", \"questionText\": \"Which reaction is commonly used to increase the carbon chain of an aldehyde by one carbon atom?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-31\", \"title\": \"Question 29\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-233\", \"text\": \"Acetal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-234\", \"text\": \"Cyanohydrin\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-235\", \"text\": \"Oxime\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-236\", \"text\": \"Hydrazone\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"HCN adds nucleophilically across the carbonyl group to form a cyanohydrin.\", \"questionText\": \"Which compound is formed when acetone reacts with HCN?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-32\", \"title\": \"Question 30\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-241\", \"text\": \"NaBH₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-242\", \"text\": \"LiAlH₄\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-243\", \"text\": \"Tollens\' reagent\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-244\", \"text\": \"H₂/Ni\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Tollens\' reagent oxidizes aldehydes to carboxylates while depositing a silver mirror. Ordinary ketones do not give this test.\", \"questionText\": \"Which reagent distinguishes aldehydes from ketones because it oxidizes aldehydes but generally not ketones?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-33\", \"title\": \"Question 31\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-249\", \"text\": \"CH₃CHO\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-250\", \"text\": \"C₆H₅OH\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-251\", \"text\": \"CH₃CH₂OH\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-252\", \"text\": \"CH₃COCH₃\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Ethanol contains only sp³-hybridized carbon atoms. The other three compounds contain either a carbonyl carbon or an aromatic ring with sp² carbons.\", \"questionText\": \"Which compound does not contain an sp²-hybridized carbon atom?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-34\", \"title\": \"Question 32\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-257\", \"text\": \"Propanal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-258\", \"text\": \"Acetophenone\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-259\", \"text\": \"Benzaldehyde\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-260\", \"text\": \"Benzyl alcohol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Benzyl alcohol contains a hydroxyl (–OH) group attached to a CH₂ group, not a carbonyl (C=O) group.\", \"questionText\": \"Which compound does not contain a carbonyl group?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-35\", \"title\": \"Question 33\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-265\", \"text\": \"Phenol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-266\", \"text\": \"p-Cresol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-267\", \"text\": \"Cyclohexanol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-268\", \"text\": \"Catechol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"In cyclohexanol, –OH is attached to an sp³ carbon. In phenols, the –OH group is directly attached to an aromatic sp² carbon.\", \"questionText\": \"Which compound has the hydroxyl group attached to an sp³-hybridized carbon atom?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-36\", \"title\": \"Question 34\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-273\", \"text\": \"But-2-enal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-274\", \"text\": \"Pent-2-enal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-275\", \"text\": \"3-Methylbut-2-enal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-276\", \"text\": \"Propanone\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Propanone has no C=C double bond. The other compounds contain an alkene capable of E/Z isomerism.\", \"questionText\": \"Which compound cannot exhibit geometrical (E/Z) isomerism because of its carbonyl group?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-37\", \"title\": \"Question 35\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-281\", \"text\": \"Propan-2-ol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-282\", \"text\": \"Butan-2-one\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-283\", \"text\": \"2-Methylpropanal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-284\", \"text\": \"2-Oxopropanal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"A three-carbon compound cannot simultaneously have an aldehyde at C-1 and a ketone at C-2 as \\\"2-oxopropanal\\\" in the intended simple context. This name is not accepted for the expected parent structure in NEET nomenclature.\", \"questionText\": \"Which IUPAC name is incorrect?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-38\", \"title\": \"Question 36\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-289\", \"text\": \"Propanal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-290\", \"text\": \"Propanone\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-291\", \"text\": \"Allyl alcohol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-292\", \"text\": \"Propan-1-ol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Propan-1-ol has molecular formula C₃H₈O, whereas the other three compounds have formula C₃H₆O.\", \"questionText\": \"Which compound is not an isomer of molecular formula C₃H₆O?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-39\", \"title\": \"Question 37\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-297\", \"text\": \"Butan-2-ol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-298\", \"text\": \"2-Methylbutan-1-ol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-299\", \"text\": \"Pentan-2-ol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-300\", \"text\": \"2-Methylpropan-2-ol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The carbon bearing –OH is attached to three identical methyl groups, so no asymmetric carbon is present.\", \"questionText\": \"Which compound contains no chiral carbon atom?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-40\", \"title\": \"Question 38\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-305\", \"text\": \"Ethoxyethane\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-306\", \"text\": \"Propan-2-ol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-307\", \"text\": \"Propanal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-308\", \"text\": \"Phenol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Aldehyde has higher IUPAC priority than alcohol, phenol, and ether.\", \"questionText\": \"Which compound has the highest priority functional group according to IUPAC nomenclature?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-41\", \"title\": \"Question 39\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-313\", \"text\": \"Phenol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-314\", \"text\": \"Benzene-1,2-diol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-315\", \"text\": \"Benzyl alcohol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-316\", \"text\": \"Benzene-1,4-diol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"In benzyl alcohol, –OH is attached to the side-chain carbon, not directly to the aromatic ring, so it is an alcohol, not a phenol.\", \"questionText\": \"Which compound is not correctly classified as an aromatic hydroxy compound?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-42\", \"title\": \"Question 40\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-321\", \"text\": \"Ethane-1,2-diol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-322\", \"text\": \"Benzene-1,3-diol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-323\", \"text\": \"2-Hydroxyethanal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-324\", \"text\": \"Propane-1,3-diol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"2-Hydroxyethanal contains both an aldehyde (–CHO) and an alcohol (–OH) functional group. The other compounds contain only hydroxyl groups.\", \"questionText\": \"Which compound contains two different functional groups?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-43\", \"title\": \"Question 41\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-329\", \"text\": \"Phenol &gt; Ethanol &gt; tert-Butanol &gt; Water\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-330\", \"text\": \"Water &gt; Phenol &gt; Ethanol &gt; tert-Butanol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-331\", \"text\": \"Phenol &gt; Water &gt; Ethanol &gt; tert-Butanol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-332\", \"text\": \"Water &gt; Ethanol &gt; Phenol &gt; tert-Butanol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Phenol is most acidic because the phenoxide ion is resonance stabilized. Water is more acidic than alcohols. Among alcohols, ethanol is slightly more acidic than tert-butanol because the bulky alkyl groups in tert-butanol donate electrons (+I effect), destabilizing the alkoxide ion.\", \"questionText\": \"Arrange the following compounds in decreasing order of acidity:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-44\", \"title\": \"Question 42\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-337\", \"text\": \"Benzaldehyde\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-338\", \"text\": \"Acetaldehyde\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-339\", \"text\": \"Acetone\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-340\", \"text\": \"Benzophenone\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Reactivity towards nucleophilic addition decreases as: Aliphatic aldehydes &gt; Aromatic aldehydes &gt; Aliphatic ketones &gt; Aromatic ketones. Acetaldehyde has less steric hindrance and only one electron-donating alkyl group, making the carbonyl carbon highly electrophilic.\", \"questionText\": \"Which compound undergoes nucleophilic addition reaction most rapidly?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-45\", \"title\": \"Question 43\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-345\", \"text\": \"Ethanol (carbon bonded to OH)\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-346\", \"text\": \"Phenol (carbon bonded to OH)\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-347\", \"text\": \"Formaldehyde (carbonyl carbon)\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-348\", \"text\": \"Acetylene\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"s-character increases as sp (50%) &gt; sp² (33%) &gt; sp³ (25%). The carbon atoms in acetylene are sp hybridized, so they possess the highest s-character.\", \"questionText\": \"Which compound contains the carbon atom with the highest percentage of s-character?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-46\", \"title\": \"Question 44\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-353\", \"text\": \"C–O bond in ethanol is shorter than C–O bond in phenol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-354\", \"text\": \"C–O bond in phenol is shorter than C–O bond in ethanol\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-355\", \"text\": \"C–O bond lengths in ethanol and phenol are equal\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-356\", \"text\": \"C–O bond in aldehydes is shorter than C=O bond\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"In phenol, the lone pair on oxygen participates in resonance with the benzene ring, giving the C–O bond partial double-bond character, making it shorter than the pure single C–O bond in ethanol.\", \"questionText\": \"Which statement is correct regarding C–O bond length?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1785079144449-47\", \"title\": \"Question 45\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-361\", \"text\": \"Formaldehyde\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-362\", \"text\": \"Acetaldehyde\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-363\", \"text\": \"Acetone\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1785079144449-364\", \"text\": \"Benzaldehyde\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Alkyl groups donate electrons by the +I effect, reducing the positive charge on the carbonyl carbon. Acetone has two methyl groups, making its carbonyl carbon the least electrophilic among the given compounds and therefore the least reactive toward nucleophilic addition.\", \"questionText\": \"Which of the following has the least electrophilic carbonyl carbon?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}], \"storageKey\": \"68\", \"totalPoints\": 45}',1,5,'2026-07-26 15:19:11','2026-07-26 15:19:40',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-26 15:19:40',1),(63,69,'{\"testId\": 69, \"savedAt\": \"2026-08-17T19:20:04.768Z\", \"version\": 1, \"questions\": [{\"id\": \"question-1786994403639-4\", \"title\": \"Question 3\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1786994403639-13\", \"text\": \"To produce electricity\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1786994403639-14\", \"text\": \"To process data\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1786994403639-15\", \"text\": \"To measure temperature\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1786994403639-16\", \"text\": \"To generate sound\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"A computer\'s primary function is to process data and convert it into useful information.\", \"questionText\": \"What is the main function of a computer?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1786994403639-5\", \"title\": \"Question 4\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1786994403639-21\", \"text\": \"RAM\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1786994403639-22\", \"text\": \"Hard Disk\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1786994403639-23\", \"text\": \"CPU\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1786994403639-24\", \"text\": \"Monitor\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"The CPU (Central Processing Unit) performs calculations and executes instructions, so it is commonly called the brain of the computer.\", \"questionText\": \"What is commonly called the brain of a computer?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionIm