acting for 2 s is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-24\", \"title\": \"Question 22\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-177\", \"text\": \"kinetic energy only\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-178\", \"text\": \"momentum only\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-179\", \"text\": \"both kinetic energy and momentum\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-180\", \"text\": \"neither\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Momentum is conserved in all collisions, but kinetic energy is not conserved in inelastic collisions.\", \"questionText\": \"In a perfectly inelastic collision, the conserved quantity is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-25\", \"title\": \"Question 23\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-185\", \"text\": \"5 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-186\", \"text\": \"10 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-187\", \"text\": \"20 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-188\", \"text\": \"25 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 10 × 5). v = √100 = 10 m/s.\", \"questionText\": \"A 2 kg ball dropped from 5 m hits the ground. Taking g = 10 m/s², its speed just before impact is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-26\", \"title\": \"Question 24\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-193\", \"text\": \"0.1\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-194\", \"text\": \"0.2\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-195\", \"text\": \"0.5\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-196\", \"text\": \"2\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Normal force N = mg = 100 N. Since f = μN, μ = f/N = 20/100 = 0.2.\", \"questionText\": \"A 10 kg block requires a horizontal force of 20 N to slide at constant speed. The coefficient of kinetic friction is (g = 10 m/s²):\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-27\", \"title\": \"Question 25\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-201\", \"text\": \"2.5 m/s²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-202\", \"text\": \"10 m/s²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-203\", \"text\": \"12.5 m/s²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-204\", \"text\": \"25 m/s²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"a = v²/r = 5²/2 = 25/2. a = 12.5 m/s².\", \"questionText\": \"Centripetal acceleration of an object moving at 5 m/s in a circle of radius 2 m is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-28\", \"title\": \"Question 26\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-209\", \"text\": \"kg m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-210\", \"text\": \"kg m²/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-211\", \"text\": \"kg m/s²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-212\", \"text\": \"N/m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Angular momentum L = mvr, with units kg·m/s·m. Thus its SI unit is kg m²/s.\", \"questionText\": \"The SI unit of angular momentum is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-29\", \"title\": \"Question 27\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-217\", \"text\": \"2 rad/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-218\", \"text\": \"3 rad/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-219\", \"text\": \"6 rad/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-220\", \"text\": \"9 rad/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"ω = αt = 2 × 3. ω = 6 rad/s.\", \"questionText\": \"A wheel starts from rest with angular acceleration 2 rad/s². Its angular speed after 3 s is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-30\", \"title\": \"Question 28\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-225\", \"text\": \"potential energy\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-226\", \"text\": \"kinetic energy\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-227\", \"text\": \"total mechanical energy\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-228\", \"text\": \"momentum\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Net work done on an object equals its change in kinetic energy. This is the work-energy theorem.\", \"questionText\": \"The work-energy theorem states that net work done equals change in:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-31\", \"title\": \"Question 29\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-233\", \"text\": \"maximum\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-234\", \"text\": \"zero\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-235\", \"text\": \"equal to horizontal velocity\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-236\", \"text\": \"negative\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"At maximum height, the projectile momentarily stops rising. Vertical velocity becomes zero.\", \"questionText\": \"At the highest point of a projectile, its vertical velocity is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-32\", \"title\": \"Question 30\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-241\", \"text\": \"30°\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-242\", \"text\": \"45°\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-243\", \"text\": \"60°\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-244\", \"text\": \"90°\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Range R = (u² sin 2θ)/g is maximum when sin 2θ = 1. This occurs at θ = 45°.\", \"questionText\": \"The range of a projectile is maximum at launch angle:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-33\", \"title\": \"Question 31\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-249\", \"text\": \"the same body\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-250\", \"text\": \"different bodies\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-251\", \"text\": \"the same direction\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-252\", \"text\": \"always stationary bodies\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Action and reaction forces act on two different bodies. They do not cancel each other.\", \"questionText\": \"Newton\'s third law action and reaction forces act on:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-34\", \"title\": \"Question 32\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-257\", \"text\": \"zero acceleration\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-258\", \"text\": \"acceleration toward the center\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-259\", \"text\": \"tangential acceleration\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-260\", \"text\": \"acceleration away from the center\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Direction of velocity changes continuously, so there is centripetal acceleration directed toward the center.\", \"questionText\": \"An object moving in a circle at constant speed has:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-35\", \"title\": \"Question 33\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-265\", \"text\": \"10 N\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-266\", \"text\": \"98 N\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-267\", \"text\": \"1000 N\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-268\", \"text\": \"1 N\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Weight W = mg = 10 × 9.8. W ≈ 98 N.\", \"questionText\": \"A 10 kg object on Earth weighs approximately:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-36\", \"title\": \"Question 34\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-273\", \"text\": \"only at rest\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-274\", \"text\": \"moving with constant velocity\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-275\", \"text\": \"accelerating\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-276\", \"text\": \"changing direction\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Zero net force means no acceleration. The object can be at rest or moving with constant velocity.\", \"questionText\": \"If the net force on an object is zero, the object may be:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-37\", \"title\": \"Question 35\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-281\", \"text\": \"half\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-282\", \"text\": \"double\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-283\", \"text\": \"four times\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-284\", \"text\": \"same\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"T = 2π√(m/k), so T ∝ √m. √4 = 2, so period doubles.\", \"questionText\": \"If the mass on a spring is quadrupled, the period of oscillation becomes:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-38\", \"title\": \"Question 36\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-289\", \"text\": \"transverse\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrec