, \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-48\", \"title\": \"Question 46\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-369\", \"text\": \"diverging lens\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-370\", \"text\": \"converging lens\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-371\", \"text\": \"cylindrical lens\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-372\", \"text\": \"plane lens\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"A convex lens is thicker at the center and converges parallel light rays. It is converging.\", \"questionText\": \"A convex lens is a:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-49\", \"title\": \"Question 47\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-377\", \"text\": \"zero\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-378\", \"text\": \"infinite\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-379\", \"text\": \"1 m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-380\", \"text\": \"negative\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"A plane mirror does not converge or diverge light. Its focal length is considered infinite.\", \"questionText\": \"The focal length of a plane mirror is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-50\", \"title\": \"Question 48\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-385\", \"text\": \"upright and smaller\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-386\", \"text\": \"inverted and larger\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-387\", \"text\": \"upright and larger\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-388\", \"text\": \"inverted and smaller\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Negative magnification means the image is inverted. Magnitude 2 means image is twice the object size.\", \"questionText\": \"A lens magnification m = −2 means the image is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-51\", \"title\": \"Question 49\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-393\", \"text\": \"radio\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-394\", \"text\": \"visible light\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-395\", \"text\": \"X-rays\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-396\", \"text\": \"infrared\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"X-rays have higher frequency than visible, infrared, and radio waves. The electromagnetic spectrum order is radio, infrared, visible, X-rays.\", \"questionText\": \"Among the following, the electromagnetic wave with the highest frequency is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-52\", \"title\": \"Question 50\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-401\", \"text\": \"decreases\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-402\", \"text\": \"increases\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-403\", \"text\": \"remains unchanged\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-404\", \"text\": \"becomes zero\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Fringe spacing β = λD/d, so β ∝ λ. Longer wavelength increases fringe spacing.\", \"questionText\": \"In Young\'s double-slit experiment, bright fringe spacing increases if the wavelength of light:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-53\", \"title\": \"Question 51\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-409\", \"text\": \"distance\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-410\", \"text\": \"square of distance\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-411\", \"text\": \"inverse distance\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-412\", \"text\": \"inverse square of distance\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Electric force F ∝ 1/r² between two point charges. This is the inverse square law.\", \"questionText\": \"Coulomb\'s law says the force between two point charges is proportional to:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-54\", \"title\": \"Question 52\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-417\", \"text\": \"N/C\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-418\", \"text\": \"V/m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-419\", \"text\": \"both A and B\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-420\", \"text\": \"C/N\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Electric field is force per charge (N/C) and potential gradient (V/m). Both units are equivalent.\", \"questionText\": \"The SI unit of electric field is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-55\", \"title\": \"Question 53\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-425\", \"text\": \"positive\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-426\", \"text\": \"negative\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-427\", \"text\": \"zero\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-428\", \"text\": \"infinite\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Like charges repel, so work must be done to bring them together. Their potential energy is positive.\", \"questionText\": \"The electric potential energy of two like charges is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-56\", \"title\": \"Question 54\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-433\", \"text\": \"2.5 μJ\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-434\", \"text\": \"10 μJ\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-435\", \"text\": \"0.4 μJ\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-436\", \"text\": \"7 μJ\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Energy U = qV = 2 μC × 5 V. U = 10 μJ.\", \"questionText\": \"A 2 μC charge moves through a potential difference of 5 V. The energy gained is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-57\", \"title\": \"Question 55\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-441\", \"text\": \"increased\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-442\", \"text\": \"decreased\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-443\", \"text\": \"kept constant\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-444\", \"text\": \"doubled\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"C = εA/d, so capacitance is inversely proportional to separation. Decreasing d increases C.\", \"questionText\": \"Capacitance of a parallel plate capacitor increases if the plate separation is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-58\", \"title\": \"Question 56\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-449\", \"text\": \"2 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-450\", \"text\": \"4 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-451\", \"text\": \"8 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-452\", \"text\": \"16 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"In series, resistances add: R = 4 + 4. R = 8 Ω.\", \"questionText\": \"Equivalent resistance of two 4 Ω resistors in series is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-59\", \"title\": \"Question 57\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-457\", \"text\": \"2 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-458\", \"text\": \"4 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-459\", \"text\": \"8 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-460\", \"text\": \"16 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"For two equal resistors in parallel, R = 4/2. R = 2 Ω.\", \"questionText\": \"Equivalent resistance of two 4 Ω resistors in parallel is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-60\", \"title\": \"Question 58\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-465\", \"text\": \"V = I/R\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-466\", \"text\": \"V = IR\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-467\", \"text\": \"V = R/I\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-468\", \"text\": \"V = I²R\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Ohm\'s law states voltage equals current times resistance. V = IR.\", \"questionText\": \"Ohm\'s law is expressed as:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-61\", \"title\": \"Question 59\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-473\", \"text\": \"6 W\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-474\", \"text\": \"9 W\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-475\", \"text\": \"12 W\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-476\", \"text\": \"18 W\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"P = I²R = 2² × 3 = 4 × 3. P = 12 W.\", \"questionText\": \"Power dissipated by a 3 Ω resistor carrying 2 A is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006753-62\", \"title\": \"Question 60\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-481\", \"text\": \"0.5 A\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-482\", \"text\": \"2 A\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006753-483\", \"text\": \"6 A\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorr