\"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006754-99\", \"title\": \"Question 97\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-777\", \"text\": \"maximum\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-778\", \"text\": \"zero\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-779\", \"text\": \"infinite\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-780\", \"text\": \"dependent on shape\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Charges reside on the surface, so the net electric field inside a conductor is zero in equilibrium.\", \"questionText\": \"In electrostatic equilibrium, the electric field inside a charged conductor is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006754-100\", \"title\": \"Question 98\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-785\", \"text\": \"ampere\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-786\", \"text\": \"coulomb\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-787\", \"text\": \"volt\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-788\", \"text\": \"ohm\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Electric charge is measured in coulombs. One coulomb is charge transported by 1 A in 1 s.\", \"questionText\": \"The SI unit of electric charge is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006754-101\", \"title\": \"Question 99\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-793\", \"text\": \"30 Wh\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-794\", \"text\": \"60 Wh\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-795\", \"text\": \"120 Wh\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-796\", \"text\": \"240 Wh\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Energy E = Pt = 60 × 2. E = 120 Wh.\", \"questionText\": \"A 60 W bulb operated for 2 hours uses energy of:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068006754-102\", \"title\": \"Question 100\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-801\", \"text\": \"maximum\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-802\", \"text\": \"minimum\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-803\", \"text\": \"zero\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068006754-804\", \"text\": \"infinite\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"At resonance, inductive and capacitive reactances cancel. Impedance equals resistance, which is minimum.\", \"questionText\": \"In a series RLC circuit at resonance, the impedance is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-103\", \"title\": \"Question 101\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059754-809\", \"text\": \"50 m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059754-810\", \"text\": \"100 m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059754-811\", \"text\": \"200 m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059754-812\", \"text\": \"400 m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Average speed = (0 + 20)/2 = 10 m/s. Distance = average speed × time = 10 × 10 = 100 m.\", \"questionText\": \"A car accelerates uniformly from rest to 20 m/s in 10 s. What distance does it cover?\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-104\", \"title\": \"Question 102\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-817\", \"text\": \"3 J\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-818\", \"text\": \"6 J\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-819\", \"text\": \"9 J\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-820\", \"text\": \"18 J\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"KE = 1/2 mv² = 1/2 × 2 × 3². KE = 9 J.\", \"questionText\": \"A 2 kg object moving at 3 m/s has kinetic energy of:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-105\", \"title\": \"Question 103\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-825\", \"text\": \"10 m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-826\", \"text\": \"20 m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-827\", \"text\": \"40 m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-828\", \"text\": \"80 m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Using v² = 2gh, 20² = 2 × 10 × h. h = 400/20 = 20 m.\", \"questionText\": \"A ball is thrown vertically upward with speed 20 m/s. Taking g = 10 m/s², the maximum height is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-106\", \"title\": \"Question 104\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-833\", \"text\": \"6 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-834\", \"text\": \"10 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-835\", \"text\": \"15 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-836\", \"text\": \"30 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Acceleration a = F/m = 10/2 = 5 m/s². Final speed v = at = 5 × 3 = 15 m/s.\", \"questionText\": \"A force of 10 N acts on a 2 kg mass for 3 s from rest. The final speed is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-107\", \"title\": \"Question 105\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-841\", \"text\": \"5 N\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-842\", \"text\": \"7 N\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-843\", \"text\": \"1 N\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-844\", \"text\": \"12 N\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Resultant = √(3² + 4²) = √(9 + 16). Resultant = √25 = 5 N.\", \"questionText\": \"Two perpendicular forces of 3 N and 4 N act on a body. The resultant force is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-108\", \"title\": \"Question 106\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-849\", \"text\": \"2 m/s²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-850\", \"text\": \"4 m/s²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-851\", \"text\": \"5 m/s²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-852\", \"text\": \"100 m/s²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"a = F/m = 20/5. a = 4 m/s².\", \"questionText\": \"A 5 kg block on a frictionless surface is pulled by a 20 N horizontal force. Its acceleration is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-109\", \"title\": \"Question 107\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-857\", \"text\": \"2.5 kg m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-858\", \"text\": \"14 kg m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-859\", \"text\": \"40 kg m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-860\", \"text\": \"80 kg m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Momentum p = mv = 10 × 4. p = 40 kg m/s.\", \"questionText\": \"The momentum of a 10 kg object moving at 4 m/s is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-110\", \"title\": \"Question 108\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-865\", \"text\": \"12.5 J\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-866\", \"text\": \"50 J\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-867\", \"text\": \"100 J\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-868\", \"text\": \"200 J\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Work W = Fd = 50 × 4. W = 200 J.\", \"questionText\": \"Work done by a 50 N force moving an object 4 m along the force is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-111\", \"title\": \"Question 109\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-873\", \"text\": \"10 W\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-874\", \"text\": \"40 W\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-875\", \"text\": \"100 W\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-876\", \"text\": \"1000 W\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Work = force × distance = 200 × 5 = 1000 J. Power = work/time = 1000/10 = 100 W.\", \"questionText\": \"Power needed to lift a 200 N object 5 m in 10 s is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-112\", \"title\": \"Question 110\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-881\", \"text\": \"2π rad/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-882\", \"text\": \"4π rad/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-883\", \"text\": \"6π rad/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-884\", \"text\": \"8π rad/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"120 rev/min = 2 rev/s. Angular speed = 2 × 2π = 4π rad/s.\", \"questionText\": \"A wheel rotates at 120 revolutions per minute. Its angular speed in rad/s is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-113\", \"title\": \"Question 111\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-889\", \"text\": \"2 N m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-890\", \"text\": \"5 N m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-891\", \"text\": \"10 N m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-892\", \"text\": \"20 N m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Torque τ = rF = 0.5 × 10. τ = 5 N m.\", \"questionText\": \"A 10 N force acts at a perpendicular distance of 0.5 m from a pivot. The torque is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-114\", \"title\": \"Question 112\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-897\", \"text\": \"6 kg m²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-898\", \"text\": \"9 kg m²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-899\", \"text\": \"12 kg m²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-900\", \"text\": \"18 kg m²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"I = mr² = 2 × 3². I = 18 kg m².\", \"questionText\": \"Moment of inertia of a 2 kg point mass at distance 3 m from the axis is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-115\", \"title\": \"Question 113\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-905\", \"text\": \"half\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-906\", \"text\": \"double\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-907\", \"text\": \"four times\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-908\", \"text\": \"one-fourth\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Gravitational force F ∝ 1/r². Halving r gives F\' = F/(1/2)² = 4F.\", \"questionText\": \"If the distance between two point masses is halved, the gravitational force becomes:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-116\", \"title\": \"Question 114\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-913\", \"text\": \"tension\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-914\", \"text\": \"gravity\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-915\", \"text\": \"friction\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-916\", \"text\": \"magnetic force\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"A satellite in orbit is held in circular motion by Earth\'s gravitational attraction. This gravity acts as the centripetal force.\", \"questionText\": \"The centripetal force on a satellite in circular orbit is provided by:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-117\", \"title\": \"Question 115\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-921\", \"text\": \"7.9 km/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-922\", \"text\": \"11.2 km/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-923\", \"text\": \"3.0 km/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-924\", \"text\": \"42 km/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Escape speed from Earth\'s surface is about 11.2 km/s. It depends on Earth\'s mass and radius.\", \"questionText\": \"The escape speed from Earth is approximately:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-118\", \"title\": \"Question 116\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-929\", \"text\": \"0.5 s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-930\", \"text\": \"1 s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-931\", \"text\": \"2 s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-932\", \"text\": \"3 s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"T = 2π√(L/g) = 2π√(1/10). T ≈ 1.99 s ≈ 2 s.\", \"questionText\": \"The approximate period of a simple pendulum of length 1 m on Earth (g = 10 m/s²) is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-119\", \"title\": \"Question 117\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-937\", \"text\": \"0.25 Hz\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-938\", \"text\": \"2 Hz\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-939\", \"text\": \"4 Hz\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-940\", \"text\": \"25 Hz\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Frequency f = 1/T = 1/0.25. f = 4 Hz.\", \"questionText\": \"A wave has a period of 0.25 s. Its frequency is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-120\", \"title\": \"Question 118\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-945\", \"text\": \"equilibrium\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-946\", \"text\": \"maximum displacement\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-947\", \"text\": \"midpoint\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-948\", \"text\": \"zero displacement\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Acceleration in SHM is proportional to displacement from equilibrium. It is maximum at maximum displacement.\", \"questionText\": \"In simple harmonic motion, acceleration is maximum at:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-121\", \"title\": \"Question 119\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-953\", \"text\": \"1 J\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-954\", \"text\": \"2 J\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-955\", \"text\": \"10 J\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-956\", \"text\": \"20 J\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"U = 1/2 kx² = 1/2 × 200 × (0.1)². U = 1 J.\", \"questionText\": \"A spring with k = 200 N/m is stretched by 0.1 m. The elastic potential energy stored is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-122\", \"title\": \"Question 120\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-961\", \"text\": \"30,000 kg m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-962\", \"text\": \"75 kg m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-963\", \"text\": \"300 kg m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-964\", \"text\": \"1500 kg m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"p = mv = 1500 × 20. p = 30,000 kg m/s.\", \"questionText\": \"A 1500 kg car moving at 20 m/s has momentum:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-123\", \"title\": \"Question 121\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-969\", \"text\": \"2.5 N s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-970\", \"text\": \"7 N s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-971\", \"text\": \"10 N s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-972\", \"text\": \"25 N s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Impulse J = Ft = 5 × 2. J = 10 N s.\", \"questionText\": \"Impulse delivered by a 5 N force acting for 2 s is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-124\", \"title\": \"Question 122\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-977\", \"text\": \"kinetic energy only\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-978\", \"text\": \"momentum only\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-979\", \"text\": \"both kinetic energy and momentum\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-980\", \"text\": \"neither\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Momentum is conserved in all collisions, but kinetic energy is not conserved in inelastic collisions.\", \"questionText\": \"In a perfectly inelastic collision, the conserved quantity is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-125\", \"title\": \"Question 123\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-985\", \"text\": \"5 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-986\", \"text\": \"10 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-987\", \"text\": \"20 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-988\", \"text\": \"25 m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 10 × 5). v = √100 = 10 m/s.\", \"questionText\": \"A 2 kg ball dropped from 5 m hits the ground. Taking g = 10 m/s², its speed just before impact is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-126\", \"title\": \"Question 124\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-993\", \"text\": \"0.1\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-994\", \"text\": \"0.2\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-995\", \"text\": \"0.5\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-996\", \"text\": \"2\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Normal force N = mg = 100 N. Since f = μN, μ = f/N = 20/100 = 0.2.\", \"questionText\": \"A 10 kg block requires a horizontal force of 20 N to slide at constant speed. The coefficient of kinetic friction is (g = 10 m/s²):\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-127\", \"title\": \"Question 125\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1001\", \"text\": \"2.5 m/s²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1002\", \"text\": \"10 m/s²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1003\", \"text\": \"12.5 m/s²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1004\", \"text\": \"25 m/s²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"a = v²/r = 5²/2 = 25/2. a = 12.5 m/s².\", \"questionText\": \"Centripetal acceleration of an object moving at 5 m/s in a circle of radius 2 m is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-128\", \"title\": \"Question 126\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1009\", \"text\": \"kg m/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1010\", \"text\": \"kg m²/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1011\", \"text\": \"kg m/s²\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1012\", \"text\": \"N/m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Angular momentum L = mvr, with units kg·m/s·m. Thus its SI unit is kg m²/s.\", \"questionText\": \"The SI unit of angular momentum is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-129\", \"title\": \"Question 127\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1017\", \"text\": \"2 rad/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1018\", \"text\": \"3 rad/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1019\", \"text\": \"6 rad/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1020\", \"text\": \"9 rad/s\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"ω = αt = 2 × 3. ω = 6 rad/s.\", \"questionText\": \"A wheel starts from rest with angular acceleration 2 rad/s². Its angular speed after 3 s is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-130\", \"title\": \"Question 128\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1025\", \"text\": \"potential energy\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1026\", \"text\": \"kinetic energy\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1027\", \"text\": \"total mechanical energy\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1028\", \"text\": \"momentum\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Net work done on an object equals its change in kinetic energy. This is the work-energy theorem.\", \"questionText\": \"The work-energy theorem states that net work done equals change in:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-131\", \"title\": \"Question 129\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1033\", \"text\": \"maximum\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1034\", \"text\": \"zero\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1035\", \"text\": \"equal to horizontal velocity\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1036\", \"text\": \"negative\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"At maximum height, the projectile momentarily stops rising. Vertical velocity becomes zero.\", \"questionText\": \"At the highest point of a projectile, its vertical velocity is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-132\", \"title\": \"Question 130\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1041\", \"text\": \"30°\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1042\", \"text\": \"45°\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1043\", \"text\": \"60°\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1044\", \"text\": \"90°\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Range R = (u² sin 2θ)/g is maximum when sin 2θ = 1. This occurs at θ = 45°.\", \"questionText\": \"The range of a projectile is maximum at launch angle:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-133\", \"title\": \"Question 131\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1049\", \"text\": \"the same body\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1050\", \"text\": \"different bodies\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1051\", \"text\": \"the same direction\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1052\", \"text\": \"always stationary bodies\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Action and reaction forces act on two different bodies. They do not cancel each other.\", \"questionText\": \"Newton\'s third law action and reaction forces act on:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-134\", \"title\": \"Question 132\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1057\", \"text\": \"zero acceleration\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1058\", \"text\": \"acceleration toward the center\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1059\", \"text\": \"tangential acceleration\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1060\", \"text\": \"acceleration away from the center\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Direction of velocity changes continuously, so there is centripetal acceleration directed toward the center.\", \"questionText\": \"An object moving in a circle at constant speed has:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-135\", \"title\": \"Question 133\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1065\", \"text\": \"10 N\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1066\", \"text\": \"98 N\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1067\", \"text\": \"1000 N\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1068\", \"text\": \"1 N\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Weight W = mg = 10 × 9.8. W ≈ 98 N.\", \"questionText\": \"A 10 kg object on Earth weighs approximately:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-136\", \"title\": \"Question 134\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1073\", \"text\": \"only at rest\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1074\", \"text\": \"moving with constant velocity\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1075\", \"text\": \"accelerating\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1076\", \"text\": \"changing direction\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Zero net force means no acceleration. The object can be at rest or moving with constant velocity.\", \"questionText\": \"If the net force on an object is zero, the object may be:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-137\", \"title\": \"Question 135\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1081\", \"text\": \"half\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1082\", \"text\": \"double\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1083\", \"text\": \"four times\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1084\", \"text\": \"same\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"T = 2π√(m/k), so T ∝ √m. √4 = 2, so period doubles.\", \"questionText\": \"If the mass on a spring is quadrupled, the period of oscillation becomes:\", \"questionType\": \"multip