ases\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1202\", \"text\": \"increases\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1203\", \"text\": \"remains unchanged\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1204\", \"text\": \"becomes zero\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Fringe spacing β = λD/d, so β ∝ λ. Longer wavelength increases fringe spacing.\", \"questionText\": \"In Young\'s double-slit experiment, bright fringe spacing increases if the wavelength of light:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-153\", \"title\": \"Question 151\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1209\", \"text\": \"distance\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1210\", \"text\": \"square of distance\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1211\", \"text\": \"inverse distance\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1212\", \"text\": \"inverse square of distance\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Electric force F ∝ 1/r² between two point charges. This is the inverse square law.\", \"questionText\": \"Coulomb\'s law says the force between two point charges is proportional to:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-154\", \"title\": \"Question 152\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1217\", \"text\": \"N/C\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1218\", \"text\": \"V/m\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1219\", \"text\": \"both A and B\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1220\", \"text\": \"C/N\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Electric field is force per charge (N/C) and potential gradient (V/m). Both units are equivalent.\", \"questionText\": \"The SI unit of electric field is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-155\", \"title\": \"Question 153\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1225\", \"text\": \"positive\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1226\", \"text\": \"negative\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1227\", \"text\": \"zero\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1228\", \"text\": \"infinite\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Like charges repel, so work must be done to bring them together. Their potential energy is positive.\", \"questionText\": \"The electric potential energy of two like charges is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-156\", \"title\": \"Question 154\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1233\", \"text\": \"2.5 μJ\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1234\", \"text\": \"10 μJ\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1235\", \"text\": \"0.4 μJ\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1236\", \"text\": \"7 μJ\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Energy U = qV = 2 μC × 5 V. U = 10 μJ.\", \"questionText\": \"A 2 μC charge moves through a potential difference of 5 V. The energy gained is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-157\", \"title\": \"Question 155\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1241\", \"text\": \"increased\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1242\", \"text\": \"decreased\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1243\", \"text\": \"kept constant\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1244\", \"text\": \"doubled\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"C = εA/d, so capacitance is inversely proportional to separation. Decreasing d increases C.\", \"questionText\": \"Capacitance of a parallel plate capacitor increases if the plate separation is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-158\", \"title\": \"Question 156\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1249\", \"text\": \"2 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1250\", \"text\": \"4 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1251\", \"text\": \"8 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1252\", \"text\": \"16 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"In series, resistances add: R = 4 + 4. R = 8 Ω.\", \"questionText\": \"Equivalent resistance of two 4 Ω resistors in series is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-159\", \"title\": \"Question 157\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1257\", \"text\": \"2 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1258\", \"text\": \"4 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1259\", \"text\": \"8 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1260\", \"text\": \"16 Ω\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"For two equal resistors in parallel, R = 4/2. R = 2 Ω.\", \"questionText\": \"Equivalent resistance of two 4 Ω resistors in parallel is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-160\", \"title\": \"Question 158\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1265\", \"text\": \"V = I/R\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1266\", \"text\": \"V = IR\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1267\", \"text\": \"V = R/I\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1268\", \"text\": \"V = I²R\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"Ohm\'s law states voltage equals current times resistance. V = IR.\", \"questionText\": \"Ohm\'s law is expressed as:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"questionImageUrl\": null}, {\"id\": \"question-1787068059755-161\", \"title\": \"Question 159\", \"points\": 1, \"choices\": [{\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1273\", \"text\": \"6 W\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1274\", \"text\": \"9 W\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1275\", \"text\": \"12 W\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": true}, {\"id\": \"choice-1787068059755-1276\", \"text\": \"18 W\", \"imageUrl\": null, \"isCorrect\": false}], \"collapsed\": false, \"explanation\": \"P = I²R = 2² × 3 = 4 × 3. P = 12 W.\", \"questionText\": \"Power dissipated by a 3 Ω resistor carrying 2 A is:\", \"questionType\": \"multiple_choice\", \"showExplanation\": true, \"question